    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"just-offense\" argument posits that a just society must even-handedly enforce all unjust offends to maintain the moral integrity of the legal system; ignoring illegal border crossings while punishing other crimes would be hypocritical and undermine the very rule of law it claims to uphold.\n\nThis philosophical stance argues that \"selective justice\" erodes public trust and degrades the legitimacy of the state, just as a government that enforces assault laws while turning a blind eye to illegal insurrection would be inherently unjust.\n\nTherefore, strict enforcement is not merely a policy preference but a moral necessity to ensure the law is not a \"flawed right\" (a right that is more wrong if not realized than if not violated).\n\nFor your debate team, the most effective rebuttal path involves demonstrating that a biased law does not protect the innocent, which is the primary mandate of the legal system.\n\nThis argument reframes border security not just as a political border but as a fundamental moral boundary that must be policed to avoid the \"slippery slope\" of systemic injustice.\n\nI will now correct the conclusion to be more specific.\nThe \"just-offense\" argument demands that a just society must even-handedly enforce all unjust off",
